outputs.L2:
A computer-assisted technique for visually displaying electronic raw data sets in thematic groups includes the following operations:

- obtaining multiple electronic raw data sets, each of which includes, as an attribute, at least one time specification or a unique identification characteristic;
- deriving an attribute vector for every raw data set;
- assembling an attribute matrix whose rows are formed by the respective attribute vectors;
- processing the attribute matrix by calculating clusters of the data sets, identifying associations among selected data, classifying the data sets, and/or computing summaries of the data sets;
- reducing the processing results to two dimensions;
- assigning the dimensionally reduced results to positions within a two-dimensional result space;
- forming a three-dimensional result space by supplementing that two-dimensional space with a third dimension corresponding to the time specification or unique identification characteristic; and
- producing a visual 3-D display of the resulting space using graphical representations of the raw data sets being visualized.

counterfactuals.output:
**1. A computer-implemented method for visualizing electronic raw data sets, the method comprising:**

receiving, by one or more processors, a plurality of electronic raw data sets;

generating, for each electronic raw data set, a property vector comprising values of a plurality of properties extracted from or associated with the electronic raw data set;

constructing a matrix in which the property vectors are represented as respective rows or columns;

performing a matrix-based analysis of the matrix to determine relationships among the electronic raw data sets;

applying a dimensionality-reduction operation to the matrix, based at least in part on the determined relationships, to map each property vector to first and second spatial coordinates;

assigning to each electronic raw data set a third spatial coordinate based on at least one of a time value associated with the electronic raw data set or a unique identifier associated with the electronic raw data set;

classifying the electronic raw data sets into thematic groups based on at least one of the property vectors, the determined relationships, or metadata associated with the electronic raw data sets;

generating a three-dimensional visualization in which each electronic raw data set is represented by a graphical element positioned according to the respective first, second, and third spatial coordinates and visually associated with a corresponding one of the thematic groups; and

causing the three-dimensional visualization to be presented by a display device.
